Connect chemical composition to a signal and expose missing information (@chemistry-observation-model: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../general-chemistry/measurement/Spectroscopic-evidence-and-calibration.lingua#r_5KF8FB97G6AMW8SNB4Y21E2ZHZ") { r_0E739CDF730C0B949DBB0338EF
Let a and b be the two species concentrations and suppose a response is modeled as y=β+s_A a+s_B b. β is a baseline and s_A,s_B are sensitivities with signal/concentration units. These coefficients must refer to a stated method, matrix, range and physical conditions. A hypothetical linear model is not a calibrated instrument. Reuse [Use a spectrum as chemical evidence with an observation model](../general-chemistry/measurement/Spectroscopic-evidence-and-calibration.lingua#r_5KF8FB97G6AMW8SNB4Y21E2ZHZ), [Judge a chemical method for a specified use and specimen](../general-chemistry/measurement/Validate-a-chemical-method.lingua#r_35SBSG78BYDMNMGSEKHB7PGZBR), the shared metrology definitions and [Solve simultaneous constraints without losing their solution set](../../mathematics/core/Solve-simultaneous-constraints-without-losing-their-solution-set.lingua#r_03FC1F97250326F9B36A1B2F6B).

If an independently known total C=a+b is valid, substitute a=C−b to get y=β+s_A C+(s_B−s_A)b. When s_A=s_B, the signal is constant β+s_A C for every composition; its stability says nothing about whether A is becoming B. Repeating that response cannot identify b. If the sensitivities differ and β,C are known, solve b=(y−β−s_A C)/(s_B−s_A) and a=C−b. Check 0≤b≤C and substitute both recovered concentrations back into the original relation.

For normalized concentrations C=1, β=1/5 and sensitivities (2,2), the signal is always 11/5. With sensitivities (1,3), it becomes 6/5+2b. The reversible model’s first and second half-relaxation states b=3/8 and 9/16 yield signals 39/20 and 93/40. These are original stipulated response units, not real absorption coefficients or instrument data.

A nonzero sensitivity difference gives algebraic identifiability, but a very small difference amplifies uncertainty. With all other inputs held fixed, δb=δy/(s_B−s_A). Thus decreasing the sensitivity difference from 1 to 1/100 multiplies the concentration effect of the same response error by one hundred. This is a conditional hard-bound calculation, not a full uncertainty budget; uncertain total, baseline, sensitivities and their dependence need additional terms and evidence.

If β is unknown, a single signal and the known total still leave ambiguity. At sensitivities (1,3), b=3/8 with β=1/5 and b=1/4 with β=9/20 both produce 39/20. A justified blank can constrain baseline, while matrix-specific interference may remain. If total is also unknown, at least two independent response relations with appropriate known baselines are needed to solve two concentrations; proportional sensitivity rows add no independent information. More unknown nuisance effects need more evidence, not merely more equations copied from the same measurement.

A spectral match can support an identity without determining mixture concentration or excluding all alternatives. Absorbance uses −log₁₀(I/I₀), now mathematically prepared, but linear absorbance versus concentration requires further physical and chemical conditions. Keep the core’s affine example separate from a claim that every signal follows that law. Harvey’s standardization discussion supplies measurement context; use shared metrology to distinguish calibration, adjustment and validation even when textbook prose combines calibration with an adjustment example.

**Read and reconstruct:** [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../general-chemistry/sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 5’s signal/sensitivity discussion, then the bounded common readings in [Use a spectrum as chemical evidence with an observation model](../general-chemistry/measurement/Spectroscopic-evidence-and-calibration.lingua#r_5KF8FB97G6AMW8SNB4Y21E2ZHZ) and [Judge a chemical method for a specified use and specimen](../general-chemistry/measurement/Validate-a-chemical-method.lingua#r_35SBSG78BYDMNMGSEKHB7PGZBR). Derive the blind and informative responses, recover the two stated compositions, and show the baseline ambiguity exactly. Specify independent evidence for each assumed coefficient. Explain why a good calibration fit does not demonstrate selectivity in every sample matrix.

**Continue:** [Use a spectrum as chemical evidence with an observation model](../general-chemistry/measurement/Spectroscopic-evidence-and-calibration.lingua#r_5KF8FB97G6AMW8SNB4Y21E2ZHZ).

